# The conditional weak-closure contradiction: proved intermediate steps

The normal-intersection argument constructs a faithful action of the conjugate
T_C^k on L fixing T_L pointwise. It also constructs its genuine intersection
setup and proves z ∈ [T,T] from the marked reflection and normalizer fusion.

Final assembly still requires the Burnside normalizer orbit, the reduction
|T_C| = 2 from the projective pointwise centralizer and A₆ generation exception,
and the derived/Frattini contradiction using actual projective or Aut(A₆)
Sylow geometry. No weak-closure theorem is claimed until those steps have
been assembled. Intrinsic model and explicitly permitted earlier source
inputs remain the responsibility of the parent source-theorem assembly.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, combined manuscript of 1 March 2004,
Lemma 16.4.6, printed p.1185, first two paragraphs.
